{"t": 32.794, "event": "MessageDisplay", "liveness": {"CLAUDE_PID": 120497, "hook_ppid_chain": ["sh", "claude"], "grandparent_is_claude_pid": true}, "input": {"session_id": "2ee78528-84d6-4642-a9f7-f84653fd0fd3", "transcript_path": "/home/user/.claude/projects/-tmp-spike-proj/2ee78528-84d6-4642-a9f7-f84653fd0fd3.jsonl", "cwd": "/tmp/spike/proj", "scratchpad_dir": "/tmp/claude-uid/-tmp-spike-proj/2ee78528-84d6-4642-a9f7-f84653fd0fd3/scratchpad", "prompt_id": "6aed3a23-86e6-4bc0-a03d-f6205fddedc6", "hook_event_name": "MessageDisplay", "turn_id": "8dbbd928-dd72-4cc4-a2ba-3e52f3ce5f1a", "message_id": "7c56a016-e837-471e-99e1-bd6f05ece4f0", "index": 1, "final": false, "delta": "Agnes Mallory checked the log before she checked the sky. It sat on the shelf above the chart table, its green cloth gone soft at the corners from thirty-one years of her thumbs. Every evening she opened it to the new page and wrote the same things in the same order: the date, the hour, the wind by compass point, the barometer in inches because the old instrument had never been converted, and a line on visibility. *Clear. Haze over the mainland. Fog banking to the west.* Once, in 1994, after a pod of whales had come through the channel at dawn, she had written a single word, *Whales*, and had been faintly embarrassed by it ever since.\n\n"}}
